1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,058
This is a stress run, not a demonstration.

2
00:00:03,058 --> 00:00:05,897
Two real recordings are loaded at once:

3
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:10,264
a hundred and forty five thousand frames from a J1939 truck,

4
00:00:10,264 --> 00:00:14,996
and a hundred and fifty five thousand from a two-channel car
log.

5
00:00:14,996 --> 00:00:20,165
Three hundred thousand frames, one hundred and fifty eight
identifiers,

6
00:00:20,165 --> 00:00:24,387
eleven bit and twenty nine bit together on three bus tags.

7
00:00:24,387 --> 00:00:26,280
It took 8 seconds to load.

8
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:30,257
The sniffer has to fold all of that into one row per message.

9
00:00:30,257 --> 00:00:32,083
It did it in 0 milliseconds.

10
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:38,016
The frames table shows a capped window of a thousand rows
rather than drawing three hundred

11
00:00:38,016 --> 00:00:42,384
thousand, which is what keeps it usable while a capture is
running.

12
00:00:42,384 --> 00:00:47,834
Every identifier in the truck half decodes as a J1939
parameter group.

13
00:00:47,834 --> 00:00:55,229
The same scan on a marine recording reports NMEA 2000 instead,
from the same identifier layout.

14
00:00:55,229 --> 00:00:57,020
Fault codes decode too:

15
00:00:57,020 --> 00:01:01,224
this truck reports its lamps and an empty active list.

16
00:01:01,224 --> 00:01:07,409
Engine speed is the one value on this bus that can be judged
without knowing the vehicle.

17
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:12,344
J1939 puts it in bytes three and four of the engine controller
message,

18
00:01:12,344 --> 00:01:14,290
a quarter of an rpm per bit.

19
00:01:14,290 --> 00:01:19,224
The preview decodes real frames through that definition as it
is typed.

20
00:01:19,224 --> 00:01:25,034
Plotted over the whole log, it runs between nine hundred and
thirteen and one thousand seven

21
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:26,675
hundred and sixty two rpm.

22
00:01:26,675 --> 00:01:30,275
That is an engine idling and working, which is the point:

23
00:01:30,275 --> 00:01:34,632
the number is checkable against the world, not just against
the code.

24
00:01:34,632 --> 00:01:40,913
Across all one hundred and fifty eight messages the detector
reports just four counter bytes,

25
00:01:40,913 --> 00:01:42,871
and that is the right answer.

26
00:01:42,871 --> 00:01:48,882
J1939 has no standard rolling counter, unlike the car buses
these heuristics were written

27
00:01:48,882 --> 00:01:49,422
against.

28
00:01:49,422 --> 00:01:52,934
Two of the four are on the car's diagnostic replies.

29
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:59,282
One is the truck's transport protocol, whose first byte is a
sequence number by specification.

30
00:01:59,282 --> 00:02:03,672
The detector found that without being told what the protocol
was.

31
00:02:03,672 --> 00:02:09,353
Entropy boundaries over a hundred and forty five thousand
frames take a second and a bit,

32
00:02:09,353 --> 00:02:12,162
and suggest a hundred and three field edges.

33
00:02:12,162 --> 00:02:15,864
They are candidates ranked by a match fraction, not proof.

34
00:02:15,864 --> 00:02:18,680
Through all of this nothing was transmitted.

35
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:22,648
No bus was opened at any point, the transmit gate stayed shut,

36
00:02:22,648 --> 00:02:26,616
and the run asserts both at the end rather than assuming them.

37
00:02:26,616 --> 00:02:28,707
Twenty nine checks, all passing.

38
00:02:28,707 --> 00:02:32,953
Every figure on this card came out of the run that just
finished,

39
00:02:32,953 --> 00:02:37,200
including the ones that are only fast because the work is
capped.
